[00:00] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of CNC machine tools.
AUDIO: 'I like to build stuff. I like to create things, often things that have never existed before. It\'s basically like being a wizard. You can conjure up something from your mind. You usually need more than just your mind. You need a welder or a printer or at least some macaroni and glue. The limit is usually'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the tools you have at your disposal. And there are a tremendous amount of tools in the world, but the really good ones are often locked behind closed doors, unavailable to mere plebs like you and me. There are shops that can laser cut'

[00:24] [scene:person]
VISUAL: Two men, including the creator, in a podcast setting.
AUDIO: 'some metal for you or CNC a part, but most of them won\'t unless you need 50 of them and are ready to spend 20 grand. But a few years ago, that started to change. Some people realize that you can actually make money selling individual'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'parts to individuals. You can make a lot of money. Send has supported a lot of the builds on this channel, but this is not a sponsored video. They invited me to be on their podcast, and I said yes, but only if I could get a shop tour'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'while I\'m out there, and only if I could make a video. But this is not really a video about send, cut, send. This is a video about building things, about creating things, and about how right now is a pretty amazing time to be creating'

[01:01] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Bundles of metal tubes, likely raw materials or finished parts.
AUDIO: 'things. A few years ago, it was basically impossible to get a small order from a metal fab shop in the United States. The contact page on their website goes'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nowhere. They don\'t answer the phone and they won\'t call you back unless you have a $200,000 purchase order from a major contractor. Sometimes you can convince them to take your work. They will tell you to come back in a month and when you do, they won\'t even have started on it'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'yet. A lot of the stuff I made early on in this channel was made this way and it was frustrating. But it does make sense if you have a metal shop. It takes time to quote and add customers and manage production. And if you have a few really big customers, it doesn\'t really make'

[01:40] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Animated cartoon figures representing a person with a USB drive and beer, interacting with a waterjet machine.
AUDIO: 'sense to take on small jobs that won\'t make you much money. If you wanted something laser cut 15 years ago, you kind of had to know a guy. Part of the reason I designed so many things in laser cut sheet metal is because I had a good rapport with the guy who ran the'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'water jet at a previous job I had. I\'d find him late in the evenings, hand him a USB drive and a case of beer, and he\'d water jet me out some metal when nobody was looking. It was basically a drug deal. But things started to change, and'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'China was the first to be part of that change. People in the US lament manufacturing going to China, but China makes it easy to make stuff. And a lot of US shops literally don\'t want your business. So, we reached this point a'

[02:17] [scene:person]
VISUAL: The creator speaking directly to the camera.
AUDIO: 'few years ago where it was cheaper and faster to get something made halfway around the world. And there\'s usually better communication, which is mind-boggling considering we\'re 12 hours off from each other and we don\'t even speak the same language. It\'s shocking'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how often I run into a company that makes it hard for me to give them money, which in turn makes it hard for me to build stuff. And I like building stuff. Jim Blossk also likes building stuff. Back in 2018, he was assembling wild'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'creations in his shop, trying to get parts fabricated, and running into the same problems as every other garage builder out there. Jim is an interesting dude. I get the sense that he kind of figured out what he wanted to do in life pretty early on. He\'s the kind of guy'

[02:54] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Large industrial laser cutting machine in a factory setting.
AUDIO: 'who sees opportunities where other people see obstacles. So, he bought a laser cutter and started offering to sell individual parts to hobbyists and makers, people in their garage like him. He went to those other metal shops and offered to take on any excess work that'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they needed done. He started to get lots of small batch orders from them. The small weird stuff that they didn\'t want to do. The laser cutter was quickly at capacity, so they got another one and another one and a bigger [clears throat] building and powder coating tools and'

[03:20] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: SendCutSend website interface showing material selection (Aluminum: 2024 T3, 5052 H32, 6061 T6, 7075 T6, MIC-6) for a custom part.
AUDIO: 'sheet metal benders and CNC mills, tapping inserts, anodizing. The company has grown significantly from that first laser cutting machine. They currently have over 20 as well as dozens of other machines. They ship 30,000 parts a month'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and they are the largest purchaser of Sour Patch Kids in the state of Nevada. [music] The first time I used Send Cut Sendend, I thought, "These guys are going to make'

[03:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a fortune." You just upload your file to their website, tell them what material you want it cut out of. Instantly, the website tells you if there are any issues with the design, what the total final price is, and how long it will take to get to you. A few days later, it'

[03:58] [scene:result]
VISUAL: Box filled with various laser-cut metal parts, some with "SendCutSend" branding.
AUDIO: 'shows up at your door. No phone calls, no RFQS, no salespeople, no waiting for quotes. An actual good customer experience from a metal shop. You can actually exchange money for goods and services. Amazing. The minimum order is'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '$39 and that includes shipping, which is great considering it takes 20 times that to get most metal shops to even respond to your email. You might think that automation is what allows for the lower costs, but there\'s not a ton of'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'automation in the shop. Not anymore than you\'d see in any modern metal shop. Parts move from station to station with a motorized cart driven by a person. Parts are removed by hand. It turns out people are pretty good at doing things. A lot of this has to do with software.'

[04:36] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A person holding a complex machined metal part.
AUDIO: 'Send is kind of a software company. When you upload a file, the software knows if it can be cut and it knows how much to charge for that part to meet the cost and margin. This is easier with laser cutting than it is for CNC because CNC'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'adds a whole new dimension of complexity. Still, the software calculates the estimated time for machining and programming and gives you a price right up front. This is vastly superior to the old way of submitting a drawing for a quote and never hearing'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anything back. It helps if you know CAD, but you don\'t need to. They have an AI builder that is remarkably good at making designs. The other trick here, aside from the software, is capacity. Metal comes in sheets that are 4 ft by 10 ft. Companies don\'t want to load up a'

[05:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '300lb sheet of steel to laser cut out one corner of parts, only to unload it and replace it with another heavy sheet of aluminum for another small corner of parts. But if 50 people are ordering parts, then they can fill the whole'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sheet and cut it in one go. When you submit a part, it gets added to what they call the bus stop. All the parts wait at the bus stop until a full bus of parts are there. Then it gets sent to the laser where a full sheet gets cut. If you have enough orders, which they'

[05:39] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Racks of sheet metal materials.
AUDIO: 'do, then the parts only have to wait at the bus stop for a few hours before getting cut. I am maybe making this sound easier than it is. The whole setup requires systems that combine different parts onto individual sheets, follow those parts through different processes,'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'combine all the parts in an order, pack, and ship. They\'ve done this with over a million orders across several different processes and dozens of machines for nearly a decade. The business has expanded to the point where they can'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have tons of different materials, racks of metal, wood, plastic, rubber, gasket. Sometimes people ask me why I don\'t buy a CNC laser or plasma cutter, and this is the real reason. I don\'t have space for racks of dozens of different'

[06:15] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A person standing next to large industrial Amada machines.
AUDIO: 'materials. And even if I did have space for it, a laser cutter is not just the laser. The laser cuts by vaporizing the metal. But that metal doesn\'t go away. It gets vacuumed out by a giant machine, separated into drums filled with metal'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dust. The laser needs nitrogen to cut right. So you also need a nitrogen supply. And Senut Sen uses a lot of nitrogen, a whole truckload a week on top of what they can pump directly out of the Reno Air. A laser cutter is a'

[06:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'boat. It\'s awesome to have, but the maintenance and repair is always more than you expect or want. A laser cutter is a tool that can expand your capabilities. But somebody else\'s laser cutter is way better. The company still focuses on those garage tinkerers, the'

[06:53] [scene:person]
VISUAL: The creator speaking directly to the camera.
AUDIO: 'people buying a handful of parts at a time, but their process is so easy and so inexpensive that half of their customers are now companies. SpaceX, Tesla, Boeing. It\'s often easier for people at these companies to order from'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Sen Cut Sendend than it is to have it done inhouse and way easier than to have it done at those other metal fab companies. Senutend has had some problems along the way. One of them is space. They are expanding into several surrounding buildings and still don\'t'

[07:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have room for all the machines that they need to fill demand, especially with CNC. The demand is super high and the only way to throttle that is by raising cost and lead times, which Jim admits that he hates doing. But they are'

[07:29] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Dealroom.co article headline: "SendCutSend raises $110M at $1B valuation in first-ever VC round".
AUDIO: 'expanding as fast as they can. There is a new building in Texas, one in Kentucky, and a brand new one in Reno that is very big and currently very empty. The other big problem they\'ve had is money. The company is bootstrapped, and so new machines only come in when'

[07:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the old machines are producing enough to pay for them. But recently, they finally got some outside investment, so capacity will start increasing. Lead times will start dropping, CNC cost will come down, and they will soon be the largest'

[07:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'purchaser of Sour Patch Kids in the world. You keep hearing about how manufacturing is going overseas and shops are closing down. And after dealing with these shops'

[08:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for years and knowing how hard they are to work with, I kind of got to the point where I thought, "Good." But it\'s not good. It\'s not good to have manufacturing 10,000 m away. People won\'t make excuses for all the'

[08:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'manufacturing going to China, government subsidies, and impossibly low wages. But Sen Cut Sendend is doing it in Reno, Nevada, and their customer base is growing faster than they can keep up. And they pay their employees well, and their quality is better. You can get'

[08:33] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of a laser cutter in operation, cutting metal.
AUDIO: 'stuff made in China for a little bit less, but you never really know what you\'re getting. The lasers are cranked up to 11, the speeds are too fast, and the material can be inconsistent. You\'ll sometimes hear about how you can get a part CNC machined in China for less than'

[08:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the material cost in the United States. But there\'s a reason that China\'s material cost is low. The cheap way of doing things is often more expensive. This is a lesson that I have personally learned at least 35 times. If you buy a good machine and good material and you'

[08:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hire good employees and you pay them well, you don\'t need a bunch of repair and maintenance and rework. Everything is kind of just easier. It turns out that you can make stuff in the United States. You just have to want people\'s business and you have to work hard at'

[09:12] [scene:other]
VISUAL: Rocket launch footage.
AUDIO: 'it. I really like the United States. We do some pretty cool things here. Or we used to. We used to build things in this country. We used to build the best things. the biggest things, the literal moonshots. But somewhere along the way,'

[09:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it got harder to do that. Regulations and outsourcing and just plain laziness. But we\'re coming back. We are building things again. The best companies are started by people who just want to solve a problem. People who really just want'

[09:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to be a customer of a company that doesn\'t exist. YouTube is kind of amazing. Anything you need to do, there is a video showing you how to do it. And if there\'s not, then you are responsible for making that video. This is actually'

[09:48] [scene:person]
VISUAL: The creator speaking directly to the camera.
AUDIO: 'kind of how I started this channel. I was making videos on how to do things that other people hadn\'t done videos on yet. That\'s basically what Sendut send did with a company. It didn\'t exist and it needed to exist, so they made it'

[10:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exist. The intention with that first laser machine was not to re-industrialize the United States or to rebuild US manufacturing capability, but they are helping to do that. They are part of a larger movement to build things here again. They just wanted to'

[10:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make it easier for people in their garage to get fabricated components. And in doing so, they\'ve helped create a manufacturing foundation that everyone else can build off of. Send, cut, send is not the only company in this space, partly because there are a lot of'

[10:25] [scene:result]
VISUAL: Close-up of custom-cut metal parts with "ALL HAIL THE ALGORITHM" text.
AUDIO: 'copycats. You\'ll hear a lot of companies say they are like send cutend or they are the send of this other process. There are also a few contemporaries that came about around the same time like Ponaco and Oshkut. I am partial to send cuts send, but I am happy that this'

[10:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'space is growing fast. None of these companies make products. Most of what comes out of the building is not functional or useful on its own. They make parts and they only make what you tell them to make. Amazon will allow you to find something you didn\'t know'

[10:52] [scene:other]
VISUAL: Wide shot of a desert landscape with buildings in the distance.
AUDIO: 'existed and hold it in your hand 2 days later. Send Cut Send will allow you to think of something that has never existed and hold it in your hands a few days later. You are the wizard. They just make it easier for you to be a'

[11:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wizard. Most of the stuff that I\'m making is not exactly reshaping the world, but there are people out there building things that will change the way we do things for the better. When we all have access to better tools, we can all make things and great ideas can come'

[11:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from anywhere. Companies like Senut Send are a stepping stool, a platform that we can all stand on to reach a little higher and drive a little faster. [music]'
